  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_1",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s1"
      },
      "text": "May 20, 2016 securingtomorrow.mcafee.com /mcafee-labs/attacks-swift-banking-system-benefit-insider-knowledge This week SWIFT sent another warning without details about another bank, this time in Vietnam that was compromised.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_5",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s5"
      },
      "text": "In the case of the Vietnamese bank, the file used for the attack is a fake version of the popular PDF reader Foxit.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_6",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s6"
      },
      "text": "The malware installs itself in the original Foxit installation directory and renames the original file to FoxltReader.exe.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_9",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s1"
      },
      "text": "As in the case of the Bangladeshi bank, the malware uses the configuration file Lmutilps32.dat, which can also be found in C:\\\\Windows\\\\temp\\WRTU\\.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_16",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s8"
      },
      "text": "In the code, we found that the malware uses the original driver fpdsdk.dll from the Foxit SDK to execute the transformation of the files.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_29",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s8"
      },
      "text": "In this case, investigators can share indicators such as MD5 sums, but because the attackers have customized their malware, sharing would be of little value.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_31",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "McAfee_SWIFT-Insider-Knowledge(05-20-2016)",
